<SddScaffold keywords="sdd, scaffold, tickets, dag, test-plan, approval" type="directive" ver="2.3">
  <Mission>
    Transform operator-approved V2 specifications into actual task tickets and indexes. Validate the
    real artifacts mechanically, obtain one independent semantic review of those actual artifacts,
    then obtain operator approval #2 for decomposition and test plan together. Do not write code.
  </Mission>

  <BeliefState>
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-stateless-flow"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-tool-invocation"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-operator-language"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-operator-agreement"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-task-id-uniqueness"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-ticket-section-names-normative"}}
    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-dag-and-ticket-boundaries"}}
    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-ticket-deduplication"}}
    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-ticket-has-bdd-and-tests"}}
    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-bootstrap-ticket-derivation"}}
  </BeliefState>

  <Contracts>
    {{> "contract/process/artifact-approval-flow"}}
    {{> "contract/process/artifact-approval-marker"}}
    {{> "contract/scaffold/task-ticket-structure"}}
    {{> "contract/process/question-rule-slim"}}
    {{> "contract/spec/decision-log-entry-format"}}
  </Contracts>

  <HaltConditions>
    | ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_SPEC_NOT_APPROVED` | Target spec set has not passed operator approval #1 |
    | `H_V2_INVALID` | A target is invalid V2; return to its authoring owner, never migrate it |
    | `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID` | Mechanical authoring check remains red after one decomposition retreat |
    | `H_TICKET_REVIEW_FINDING` | Independent reviewer found an unresolved semantic conflict |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected decomposition or test plan |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_PREFLIGHT">
      <Goal>Resolve the exact approved spec set and existing ticket topology.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Use the router snapshot plus actual specs and task indexes. Accept only V2 specs whose current
        state carries a well-formed `approved` approval #1 marker in
        `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER` format. Check its paths and use Git evidence
        plus semantic judgment for staleness; a marker is not a byte/hash gate. Missing, malformed, or
        stale approval stops with `H_SPEC_NOT_APPROVED`. Structurally invalid V2 stops with
        `H_V2_INVALID` and returns to the owning V2 authoring flow. Auto-detect `initial`
        or `extend-dag`; preserve existing IDs and
        completed tickets in extend mode. Every runtime, package-manager, or configuration
        prerequisite named by the approved set must resolve to exactly one Bootstrap Requirements row with a named Owner. Missing or ambiguous ownership returns to the owning spec before ticket derivation.
        Do not create a migration path, `.sdd-session.md`,
        scaffold-plan JSON, digest, feasibility event, or critic journal (`AX_STATELESS_FLOW`).
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_1_DERIVE">
      <Goal>Reason about a coherent ticket DAG before writing it.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Read the approved specs and derive the smallest useful tickets per
        `AX_DAG_AND_TICKET_BOUNDARIES` and `AX_TICKET_DEDUPLICATION`. Each ticket has one observable
        outcome, bounded target files, explicit dependencies, phase order, verification commands, and
        requirement references. Infrastructure providers precede consumers: package manager and
        dependency materialization precede scripts, configs, tests, or commands that consume them.

        Perform a local coherence pass in reasoning: every requirement has an owner, dependencies are
        acyclic, shared writers are serialized, no ticket invents requirements, and infrastructure is
        not fragmented without an independently verifiable outcome. Derive any necessary bootstrap
        work per `AX_BOOTSTRAP_TICKET_DERIVATION`. This pass needs no subagent and
        no intermediate JSON. It prepares the artifacts; it does not approve them.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_2_MATERIALIZE">
      <Goal>Create actual tickets and indexes.</Goal>
      <Action>
        For each derived node call exactly one applicable `sdd-new` command and fill the returned file.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="flatInfraTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner infrastructure-flat --scope &lt;scope&gt; --id &lt;ACR&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</ToolCall>
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="scopeBootstrapTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner scope-bootstrap --scope &lt;scope&gt; --id &lt;ACR&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</ToolCall>
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="moduleTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner module --scope &lt;scope&gt; --module &lt;module&gt; --id &lt;ACR&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</ToolCall>
        Consume exactly the applicable `flatInfraTaskManifest`, `scopeBootstrapTaskManifest`, or
        `moduleTaskManifest`; never call the other owner variants for that node.

        Preserve globally unique IDs per `AX_TASK_ID_UNIQUENESS` and the normative section names per
        `AX_TICKET_SECTION_NAMES_NORMATIVE`. Fill every ticket from owning spec anchors. Per
        `AX_TICKET_HAS_BDD_AND_TESTS`, the BDD/Test Plan must contain requirement IDs in
        the scenarios and mapped test cases. Happy-path-only is invalid: every behavior-bearing
        ticket contains at least one applicable negative/failure scenario, plus boundary or trust
        scenarios required by the spec. A scenario without a test mapping or a mandatory requirement
        without a scenario is incomplete.

        Create missing index skeletons with the canonical commands, then fill their tracker and DAG
        rows from the actual tickets.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="moduleIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new module-index --scope &lt;scope&gt; --module &lt;module&gt;</ToolCall>
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="scopeIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new scope-index --scope &lt;scope&gt;</ToolCall>
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="projectIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new project-index</ToolCall>
        Consume each produced `moduleIndexManifest`, `scopeIndexManifest`, and
        `projectIndexManifest` before filling its tracker and DAG rows.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_3_MECHANICAL_CHECK">
      <Goal>Prove actual ticket structure before semantic review.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run the authoring check on every created or changed ticket. It checks required sections,
        anchors, resolvable spec references, dependencies/phases, requirement-to-scenario-to-test
        traceability, and required negative scenarios. A red result is a decomposition defect: do not
        patch the failed ticket locally. Use the retreat command `return to STEP_1_DERIVE`, rederive
        the affected node boundaries/ownership from the specs, then rematerialize and check once. If
        that remains red, stop with `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID`. Do not ask a reviewer to compensate
        for malformed artifacts.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="authoringGate">npx gennady sdd-check --task &lt;ticket-path&gt; --authoring</ToolCall>
        Consume `authoringGate`; its findings explain why decomposition must retreat.

        Then run the complete structural check over actual specs, tickets, and indexes. It checks ID
        uniqueness, dependency resolution/acyclicity, tracker sync, and declared ownership/order.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityGate">npx gennady sdd-check --all .</ToolCall>
        Consume `integrityGate`; do not proceed to semantic review while it is red.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_4_INDEPENDENT_TICKET_REVIEW">
      <Goal>Judge the actual ticket set against approved specifications.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Dispatch one fresh read-only reviewer with exact paths and bytes of all changed tickets,
        indexes, and their approved specs. The reviewer checks: coherent decomposition; provider before
        consumer; no conflicting/shared writes; phases form an executable order; each requirement is
        owned; negative/failure behavior is represented; test cases would demonstrate the declared
        requirement; and no ticket adds product meaning absent from specs.

        The reviewer returns `CLEAN` or concrete findings naming ticket, section, and spec anchor. The
        reviewer does not edit. Do not retain it for execution and do not require it to consume a JSON
        plan. If a finding changes ticket meaning, edit the actual ticket, repeat STEP_3, and obtain one
        fresh review of the new actual set. An unresolved semantic conflict stops with
        `H_TICKET_REVIEW_FINDING`.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_5_OPERATOR_APPROVAL_2">
      <Goal>Approve decomposition and test plan in one decision.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present a compact card based on actual tickets: ordered ticket list with dependencies and
        outcomes; infrastructure provider/consumer order; Requirement-ID → scenario → planned test
        mapping; negative/failure scenarios; declared real verification commands; independent verdict.
        Ask `Approve tickets and test plan` or `Return for correction` per `AX_OPERATOR_AGREEMENT`. Do not ask approval for an
        abstract pre-ticket plan. On approval, write approval #2 to the narrowest owning task index in
        the human-readable `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER` format. Any later semantic ticket/index edit
        resets it to `pending`; no fingerprint, digest, or JSON approval record exists.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_6_HANDOFF">
      <Goal>Leave a directly executable durable queue.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Rerun the complete mechanical check after the approval marker is recorded.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="finalIntegrityGate">npx gennady sdd-check --all .</ToolCall>
        Consume `finalIntegrityGate`. Report exact ticket
        paths and the next command `/sdd-execute &lt;Task-ID|next|all&gt;`. Stopping here is safe because
        the whole continuation state lives in tickets, indexes, specs, Execution Logs, and Git.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>
</SddScaffold>
